d 135bp in Q2.\n- Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably in Q3, and Caldria Ratings rates it stable. Its bond route looks reliable.\n- Pellham's position is weaker. Caldria Ratings put it on negative watch after the withdrawn offering. A downgrade would raise its borrowing costs just as it loses the equity route.\n- The sources don't show Pellham's 2027 bond plans or its leverage headroom, so I can't say whether debt could cover its gap.\n\n**Equity markets**\n- Equity is a weak source. Pellham pulled its C$2.4B share offering on 14 October, and two other utility offerings were cut back in size in Q3. That points to weak investor demand across the sector, not only at Pellham.\n- I found no evidence on whether Norvane can raise equity.\n\n**Evidence that Pellham can't fund its plan**\n- Pellham's 2027 capex is planned up 18%, and it is the largest buyer of grid equipment in Caldria.\n- It has said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender, which was about C$900M.\n- That suggests it expects funding to be constrained and does not expect the bond market to cover the gap.\n\n**Implication:** Utility grid-equipment demand will likely come in below the filed 2027 capex plans, mainly because of Pellham.\n\nThis rests on two child nodes' findings: n5.r1 for debt and n6.r1 for equity.","ttft_ms":2074,"type":"result","duration_ms":45173,"uuid":"66ce009c-b643-44ce-ba49-ab3f8e4c612c","ttft_stream_ms":1568,"time_to_request_ms":63,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":6,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":6,"input_hooks":5,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":7,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":3,"client_creation":4,"request_build":4},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_